# html.oro — escaping text for HTML, written in Oro.
#
# API
# ---
#   html.escape(s)  -> str   `&` `<` `>` `"` `'` as character references
#
# One function, and it is for *generating* HTML: a value a program is about to
# put into a page — a file name, a commit message, a user's display name —
# goes through `escape` on its way in, and then it is text and not markup.
#
# **All five characters, always.** `&`, `<` and `>` are what element content
# needs; `"` and `'` are what an attribute value needs, and which quote the
# attribute was written with is not something this function can see. So there
# is no `quote=` flag: the one spelling is the one that is safe in element
# content *and* inside either kind of quoted attribute, and a caller who asks
# for less has asked for a page that breaks the first time a name contains a
# quote. (It is not safe in an *unquoted* attribute, in a `<script>` or
# `<style>` body, or in a URL attribute that could hold `javascript:` — those
# are different contexts, and no escaping function makes them safe.)
#
# The output is CPython's `html.escape(s)`, byte for byte — `&amp;`, `&lt;`,
# `&gt;`, `&quot;` and `&#x27;` — so `import html` and `html.escape(s)` is the
# same program in both languages and `corpus/core/74_html_escape.oro` is
# checked against CPython directly.
#
# **There is no `unescape`**, and that is the design rather than a gap. Reading
# HTML back means decoding every named character reference HTML5 defines —
# over two thousand of them, some without a trailing semicolon — and a decoder
# that knows only the five above is a codec with a hole in it: it turns
# `&eacute;` into itself and `&amp;eacute;` into `&eacute;`, silently. A
# program that has to parse HTML needs a parser, and this module is not one.
#
# Pure Oro: five `str.replace` calls, each a single pass in the runtime. `&` is
# replaced first, so the `&` in every reference the later calls insert is not
# escaped a second time.


# Escape `s` for use as HTML text or inside a quoted attribute value.
#
#     html.escape("<a href='x'>Tom & Jerry</a>")
#         -> "&lt;a href=&#x27;x&#x27;&gt;Tom &amp; Jerry&lt;/a&gt;"
def escape(s):
    if type(s) != str:
        raise TypeError(f"html.escape() takes str, not {type(s)}")
    return s.replace("&", "&amp;").replace("<", "&lt;").replace(">", "&gt;").replace('"', "&quot;").replace("'", "&#x27;")
